Chapter: HexWarWasm
  cites Games chapter HexWar
  cites Games chapter Rng
  cites Foreword chapter List

 The browser interface for Hex War, on the handle contract in
 games-backlog.md GAME-11. This game does not keep its state in one record
 the way the others do: a position is a list of units, a terrain map, whose
 turn it is and how far through the game we are, threaded as separate
 arguments through `hexwar-loop`. So the handle the page holds is a record
 defined here that gathers them, and stepping it is one call of
 `run-hexwar-turn` rather than a whole game.

 There are thirteen scenarios, each a config of its own. The page picks one
 by index and the roster and map come with it.

 A unit is never removed from the list. It is marked `eliminated` and stays
 in place, so the unit COUNT is fixed for a whole game and the alive count
 is what moves. That is the invariant the grader leans on, since a game
 that quietly grew or dropped a unit would still play.

 We say:

Section: The Handle

  HwState = record {
    cfg    : WargameConfig,
    units  : List Unit,
    turn   : Integer,
    active : Integer,
    rng    : Rng,
    over   : Boolean,
    opened : Boolean,
    attacks-left : Integer
  }

  hw-scenario : Integer -> WargameConfig
  hw-scenario (n) =
    if n == 0 then scenario-stalingrad
    else if n == 1 then scenario-normandy
    else if n == 2 then scenario-bulge
    else if n == 3 then scenario-panzerblitz
    else if n == 4 then scenario-gettysburg
    else if n == 5 then scenario-afrika-korps
    else if n == 6 then scenario-waterloo
    else if n == 7 then scenario-midway
    else if n == 8 then scenario-kursk
    else if n == 9 then scenario-market-garden
    else if n == 10 then scenario-guadalcanal
    else if n == 11 then scenario-austerlitz
    else scenario-blitzkrieg

Section: Copying

 A shallow copy of the unit list is not enough here. The turn writes with
 `__record-set` as well as `list-set-at`, so it mutates the unit RECORDS
 and then writes them back into the list; copying only the list leaves both
 sides pointing at the same units. Every field is copied by hand below for
 that reason.

  hw-copy-unit : Unit -> Unit
  hw-copy-unit (u) =
    Unit {
      id = u.id, type-id = u.type-id, owner = u.owner,
      q = u.q, r = u.r,
      strength = u.strength, max-strength = u.max-strength,
      movement = u.movement, max-movement = u.max-movement,
      attack = u.attack, defense = u.defense, range = u.range,
      experience = u.experience,
      disrupted = u.disrupted, eliminated = u.eliminated,
      has-moved = u.has-moved, has-attacked = u.has-attacked
    }

  hw-copy-units : List Unit, Integer, List Unit -> List Unit
  hw-copy-units (src) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length src then acc
    else hw-copy-units src (i + 1) (list-push acc (hw-copy-unit (list-at src i)))

Section: The Browser Interface

  hw-wasm-new : Integer, Integer -> HwState
  hw-wasm-new (scenario) (seed) =
    let cfg = hw-scenario (if scenario < 0 then 0 else if scenario > 12 then 12 else scenario)
    in HwState { cfg = cfg, units = hw-copy-units (cfg.unit-roster) 0 [], turn = 0, active = 0,
                 rng = rng-new seed, over = False, opened = False, attacks-left = 0 }

 A state whose turn has been opened has already had its bookkeeping done,
 so the runner takes the opened turn rather than beginning it a second
 time. A watch-only session never opens one, which is why `hw-wasm-step`
 alone plays exactly the game it always did.

  hw-over : HwState -> HwState
  hw-over (s) =
    HwState { cfg = s.cfg, units = s.units, turn = s.turn, active = s.active, rng = s.rng,
              over = True, opened = s.opened, attacks-left = 0 }

 Whether this turn is the one that ends the battle, asked BEFORE the turn
 is begun. Opening a turn asks it at the same point, so a turn that is
 opened and then stepped settles exactly as one that is only stepped: a
 side that supply attrition is about to wipe out is still alive when the
 question is put, in both.

  hw-settled : HwState -> Boolean
  hw-settled (s) =
    if s.turn >= s.cfg.turn-limit then True
    else if count-alive (s.units) 0 == 0 then True
    else count-alive (s.units) 1 == 0

  hw-wasm-step : HwState -> HwState
  hw-wasm-step (s) =
    if s.over then s
    else if s.opened == False & hw-settled s then hw-over s
    else let cfg = s.cfg
    in let r2 = rng-next (s.rng)
    in let copy = hw-copy-units (s.units) 0 []
    in let units2 = if s.opened
         then run-opened-hexwar-turn copy (cfg.terrain-data) (s.active) (cfg.map-width) (cfg.map-height) (cfg.stacking-limit) r2
         else run-hexwar-turn copy (cfg.terrain-data) (s.active) (cfg.map-width) (cfg.map-height) (cfg.stacking-limit) r2
    in let next-player = rng-mod (s.active + 1) (cfg.num-players)
    in let next-turn = if next-player == 0 then s.turn + 1 else s.turn
    in HwState { cfg = cfg, units = units2, turn = next-turn, active = next-player, rng = r2,
                 over = False, opened = False, attacks-left = 0 }

  hw-wasm-units : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-units (s) = list-length (s.units)

  hw-wasm-unit-owner : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-owner (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).owner

  hw-wasm-unit-q : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-q (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).q

  hw-wasm-unit-r : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-r (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).r

  hw-wasm-unit-strength : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-strength (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).strength

  hw-wasm-unit-max : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-max (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).max-strength

  hw-wasm-unit-dead : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-dead (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else if (list-at (s.units) i).eliminated then 1 else 0

  hw-wasm-unit-type : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-unit-type (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).type-id

  hw-wasm-alive : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-alive (s) (owner) = count-alive (s.units) owner

  hw-wasm-vp : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-vp (s) (owner) =
    ai-count-vp (s.cfg.terrain-data) (s.units) owner (s.cfg.map-width)

  hw-wasm-turn : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-turn (s) = s.turn

  hw-wasm-active : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-active (s) = s.active

  hw-wasm-turn-limit : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-turn-limit (s) = s.cfg.turn-limit

  hw-wasm-width : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-width (s) = s.cfg.map-width

  hw-wasm-height : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-height (s) = s.cfg.map-height

  hw-wasm-terrain : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-terrain (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.cfg.terrain-data) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.cfg.terrain-data) i).terrain

  hw-wasm-done : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-done (s) = if s.over then 1 else 0

  hw-wasm-winner : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-winner (s) =
    determine-winner (s.units) (s.cfg.terrain-data) (s.turn) (s.cfg.turn-limit) (s.cfg.map-width)

Section: Taking A Turn Yourself

 Opening a turn is what `run-hexwar-turn` does to its own first, and it is
 idempotent here so a page cannot spend a side's supply twice by asking
 again. Until a turn is opened its units carry last turn's movement, so a
 page opens before it reads anything it means to offer as a move.

  hw-wasm-open : HwState -> HwState
  hw-wasm-open (s) =
    if s.over then s
    else if s.opened then s
    else if hw-settled s then hw-over s
    else HwState { cfg = s.cfg,
                   units = hw-begin-turn (hw-copy-units (s.units) 0 []) (s.active) (s.cfg.map-width) (s.cfg.map-height),
                   turn = s.turn, active = s.active, rng = s.rng, over = False,
                   opened = True, attacks-left = 1 }

  hw-wasm-opened : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-opened (s) = if s.opened then 1 else 0

  hw-wasm-attacks-left : HwState -> Integer
  hw-wasm-attacks-left (s) = s.attacks-left

  hw-wasm-movement : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-movement (s) (i) =
    if i < 0 then (-1)
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then (-1)
    else (list-at (s.units) i).movement

  hw-wasm-can-move : HwState, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-can-move (s) (i) (tq) (tr) =
    if s.over then 0
    else if s.opened == False then 0
    else if hw-can-move (s.units) (s.active) i tq tr (s.cfg.terrain-data) (s.cfg.map-width) (s.cfg.map-height) (s.cfg.stacking-limit)
      then 1 else 0

  hw-wasm-has-move : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-has-move (s) (i) =
    if s.over then 0
    else if s.opened == False then 0
    else if i < 0 then 0
    else if i >= list-length (s.units) then 0
    else if (list-at (s.units) i).owner /= s.active then 0
    else if hw-unit-has-move (s.units) (s.active) i (s.cfg.terrain-data) (s.cfg.map-width) (s.cfg.map-height) (s.cfg.stacking-limit) 0
      then 1 else 0

 A REFUSAL ANSWERS THE STATE IT WAS GIVEN, unchanged and un-copied. Copying
 first and letting the engine refuse the copy is the same game and a
 different handle, so a page comparing what came back against what it sent
 could not tell a refusal from a move.

  hw-wasm-move : HwState, Integer, Integer, Integer -> HwState
  hw-wasm-move (s) (i) (tq) (tr) =
    if hw-wasm-can-move s i tq tr == 0 then s
    else HwState { cfg = s.cfg,
                   units = hw-move-unit (hw-copy-units (s.units) 0 []) i tq tr (s.cfg.terrain-data) (s.cfg.map-width),
                   turn = s.turn, active = s.active, rng = s.rng, over = False,
                   opened = True, attacks-left = s.attacks-left }

  hw-wasm-can-attack : HwState, Integer -> Integer
  hw-wasm-can-attack (s) (target) =
    if s.over then 0
    else if s.opened == False then 0
    else if s.attacks-left <= 0 then 0
    else if hw-can-attack (s.units) (s.active) target then 1 else 0

 The die is the caller's to supply, the same way the runner takes its own:
 a page that reused one seed would fight the same battle every time.

  hw-wasm-attack : HwState, Integer, Integer -> HwState
  hw-wasm-attack (s) (target) (seed) =
    if hw-wasm-can-attack s target == 0 then s
    else let r2 = rng-new seed
    in HwState { cfg = s.cfg,
                 units = hw-resolve-attack (hw-copy-units (s.units) 0 []) (s.cfg.terrain-data) target (s.active) (s.cfg.map-width) r2,
                 turn = s.turn, active = s.active, rng = r2, over = False,
                 opened = True, attacks-left = s.attacks-left - 1 }

 Ending your turn passes the seat on without running one, and settles the
 game if your own attack finished it: `hw-wasm-step` is what sets `over`
 for a side the runner plays, and nothing else would set it for yours.

  hw-wasm-end-turn : HwState -> HwState
  hw-wasm-end-turn (s) =
    if s.over then s
    else let next-player = rng-mod (s.active + 1) (s.cfg.num-players)
    in let next-turn = if next-player == 0 then s.turn + 1 else s.turn
    in let wiped = count-alive (s.units) 0 == 0 | count-alive (s.units) 1 == 0
    in let finished = wiped | next-turn >= s.cfg.turn-limit
    in HwState { cfg = s.cfg, units = s.units, turn = next-turn, active = next-player,
                 rng = s.rng, over = finished, opened = False, attacks-left = 0 }

